I see that 1.1.2 is now on CRAN and doesn't exhibit this problem,
but this is such a strange place for the code to crash.

The fault must not be due to delete[] operating on a null pointer:
first, the faulting address is not 0; secondly, the C++ standard
requires delete[] to safely do nothing in such a case.

If the fault is really due to 'this->rank_' being written, then 'this'
must be an invalid pointer... except that the 'etr::Dim' object lives on
the stack as part of etr::Array object, so the whole stack region must
have vanished from the memory map. And the code must have just
successfully written a 0 into 'this->heap_', which lives right next to
'rank_' in memory. Maybe this write was reordered first?

0x7ff811a46993 does look more like an address on the thread 0 stack
than an address on the heap, except it's some 20 gigabytes lower than a
typical end of stack area, at least compared to some example 'vmmap'
outputs I was able to find. How did this pointer come to be?

And the problem (that was previously reproducible) disappears after
changing the code to do the same thing differently. Was it a bug in
Apple Clang 14? We may never know.

